  "sls_rules": [
    "3.5-epsilon-is-not-a-break"
  ],
  "description": "A close above the level but inside 0.05 x ATR is not a break; the next candle, clear of the tolerance, is.",
  "labelling_rationale": "s4-close-through-breaks-structure-a-wick-does-not, with one candle spliced in before its break.\n\n\u00a70.4 fixes the tolerance unit: 'e = P.global.tolerance_atr x ATR (default 0.05 x ATR(14)) -- used wherever two prices are compared for equality'. \u00a73.5's second edge case applies it to breaks: 'break of a level within e: not a break (tolerance is absolute)'.\n\nNothing could show that rule before now, because nothing implemented it. `detect_bos` takes an `epsilon` and defaults it to zero, and `structure_replay` passed none -- so \u00a73.5's own noise filter was the single place in the codebase where e did not exist, while the liquidity engine derived it correctly for pools, clusters and sweeps. On the VM, 2 of 190 recorded breaks cleared their level by less than a basis point.\n\nThe level is the external high at 288, price 67. Wilder ATR at the break candle is 3.911, so e is 0.1956 and the tolerance band reaches 67.1956.\n\nIndex 301 closes at 67.1 -- above the level, inside the band. It is not a break, and that absence is the assertion. Index 302 closes at 68, clear of the band, and breaks.\n\nThe two candles are otherwise alike, which is the point: only the distance from the level separates them, and a detector comparing `Cl > level` rather than `Cl > level + e` would break on both.",
  "labelled_by": "drafted by Claude Opus 5 2026-08-23 from SLS 0.4/3.5; VERIFIED by developer 2026-08-23 -- the rule each case turns on was confirmed in plain terms before signing. Per-candle arithmetic rests on the generator's assertions and on the parent series this one extends, as with its five siblings.",
  "labelled_at": "2026-08-23",
